@@s001 Descriptive statistics to assess models
@@s002 dato:the 9 data,ok:median,ora:box Q1-Q3,due:whiskers,no:limit and outliers
@@s003 Nine sorted data: 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, <b>30</b>. One is very far from the others.
@@s004 <b>Median</b>: the middle value, the 5th of 9, that is <b>5</b>. The mean instead is 68 / 9 ≈ 7.56: the 30 pulls it up.
@@s005 <b>Q1 = 3</b> and <b>Q3 = 7</b> (25th and 75th percentile, as NumPy computes them): the box contains the central half of the data.
@@s006 IQR = Q3 − Q1 = 4. The upper limit is Q3 + 1.5 · IQR = 7 + 6 = <b>13</b> (the lower one, −3, is not needed here).
@@s007 The <b>whiskers</b> reach the last datum within the limits: 2 at the bottom, 8 at the top.
@@s008 The 30 lies beyond the limit: it is drawn on its own as an <b>outlier</b>. The box plot shows centre, dispersion and strange values at a glance.
@@s009 spento:100 noisy points,dato:a parabola for each subset of 20,ok:true function
@@s010 dato:training MSE (mean),no:test MSE (mean),ok:test MSE (median)
@@s011 Descriptive statistics to assess models
@@s012 Week 9, exercise <i>Model selection and descriptive statistics</i>, and the tutorial of week 7 on the Titanic dataset. Mean, variance, quartiles and box plot serve to describe the data. They also serve to describe <b>the results of a model trained many times</b>: how much it errs on average, how much it changes, how stable it is. Exam question <b>Q7 (b)</b>.
@@s013 The basic statistics
@@s014 Measure
@@s015 What it says
@@s016 In pandas / NumPy
@@s017 mean, <b>median</b>, mode
@@s018 the centre (the median resists outliers)
@@s019 <code>mean()</code>, <code>median()</code>, <code>mode()</code>
@@s020 min, max, range
@@s021 the extremes
@@s022 <code>min()</code>, <code>max()</code>
@@s023 <b>quartiles</b> Q1, Q3 and IQR = Q3 − Q1
@@s024 where the central 50% of the data lies
@@s025 <code>quantile([.25, .75])</code>
@@s026 variance, standard deviation
@@s027 how much they spread around the mean
@@s028 <code>var()</code>, <code>std()</code> (pandas uses n − 1, NumPy n)
@@s029 skewness
@@s030 asymmetry (long tail to the right ⇒ positive)
@@s031 <code>scipy.stats.skew</code>
@@s032 kurtosis
@@s033 how heavy the tails are
@@s034 <code>scipy.stats.kurtosis</code>
@@s035 All together: <code>df.describe()</code>. In the Titanic the price of the ticket has mean 32.2 and median 14.5, skewness 4.8: few very expensive tickets (up to 512) pull up the mean.
@@s036 In the tutorial: skewness = NaN
@@s037 For the column <code>age</code> skewness and kurtosis come out <b>NaN</b>: the column has missing data (714 values out of 891) and the scipy functions, if they find a NaN, return NaN. First one decides what to do with the <a href="w09-dati-mancanti-outlier.html">missing data</a>, then one computes.
@@s038 Building a box plot
@@s039 Quartiles, limits and whiskers computed with NumPy (<code>np.percentile</code>).
@@s040 How to read a box plot
@@s041 Half of the data lies in the box.
@@s042 Beyond the whiskers the points are drawn one by one: they are the candidate outliers.
@@s043 Two box plots that do not overlap: the two distributions are really different.
@@s044 Training the same model 100 times
@@s045 The exercise: noisy data around y = x² − 2x + 3 (100 points, x between −10 and 10). One picks at random <b>20 points</b>, trains a polynomial, repeats <b>100 times</b>. One gets 100 errors and 100 vectors of coefficients, and describes them with statistics.
@@s046 What is measured
@@s047 How much the "typical" model errs.
@@s048 How much the result depends on <b>which</b> 20 points came up: it is the <a href="w07-complessita-modello.html">variance</a> of the model, really measured.
@@s049 How they move together. In my simulation the coefficient of x² and the constant term have correlation <b>−0.68</b>: if the parabola curves more, it lowers itself to compensate.
@@s050 Data
@@s051 mean coefficients (true: 1, −2, 3)
@@s052 std dev of the constant term
@@s053 noise variance 50, no outlier
@@s054 variance 50, <b>5 outliers</b>
@@s055 variance 50, <b>15 outliers</b>
@@s056 variance <b>150</b>, no outlier
@@s057 variance <b>650</b>, no outlier
@@s058 The code of the exercise, run with my seed (<code>np.random.seed(0)</code>): your numbers will be different, the trend will not.
@@s059 Task 2: outliers and noise do not do the same thing
@@s060 In the code each outlier is added with positive sign: it pulls the whole mean curve upwards (constant term from 3 to 77) and makes the variability explode. It is a <b>systematic</b> error.
@@s061 The 100 curves widen (std dev from 1.96 to 7.05), but they are not all pushed in the same direction.
@@s062 The 100 repetitions always draw from the <b>same</b> noisy dataset: averaging between the subsets does not remove the noise of that dataset. The mean approaches the curve computed on all the 100 points, not the true one.
@@s063 100 parabolas from 100 subsets
@@s064 Training against test (Tasks 3–5)
@@s065 Degree
@@s066 MSE training (mean)
@@s067 MSE test (mean)
@@s068 MSE test (median)
@@s069 Noise variance 100, no outlier, 100 repetitions with 20 training points and 80 test points (my seed). With degree 2 (the true one): training on average 75.5 with variance 790.9, test 102.7 with variance 232.5.
@@s070 How to read it
@@s071 The model has been chosen to stay close to the training points.
@@s072 The clue of Task 4: the training has 20 points, the test 80. A mean over few points oscillates more from one repetition to another.
@@s073 Overfitting, as in <a href="w07-complessita-modello.html">week 7</a>. The mean of the test is inflated by a few repetitions in which the polynomial "explodes" outside the points drawn: here the median (or the box plot) describes the typical case better.
@@s074 The true function has no terms beyond x²: on average the extra weights stay small, but they vary a lot from one repetition to another.
@@s075 Q7 (b)
@@s076 "Explain how descriptive statistics can be used to assess model selection and the generalization ability of machine learning models." The heart: repeating the training on different subsets (as in cross-validation) and describing errors and parameters with mean, variance, correlation, box plot. Low mean = accurate model; low variance = stable model; training and test far apart = it does not generalise.
@@s077 Mean and median of the test
